WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.450
A tectonic terrane is a fault-bounded piece of crust with a

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.450
geologic history different from its neighbors.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:11.350
Look for contrasts in rock sequences, ages, fossils, structures, chemistry, or

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.270
metamorphism. The boundary is a major fault or shear zone.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.570
A terrane is a package of evidence and history, not simply

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.890
any patch of ground with a different surface color.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.640
Terranes can begin as oceanic crust, island arcs, continental fragments, oceanic

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.500
islands, or plateaus. Oceanic pillow basalt and deep-marine sediment suggest a

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.020
different setting from thick granitic continental crust.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.700
During convergence, a buoyant or thick piece can be scraped from

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.100
a descending plate and attached to the continent.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.420
This addition is accretion, commonly accompanied by deformation and mountain building.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.910
To date attachment, look for a relationship that crosses the boundary.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.450
An intrusion that cuts both terranes shows they were already together

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.430
when it formed. A single sedimentary unit deposited across their boundary

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.390
gives another younger limit. These dates constrain when joining had happened;

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.330
they do not necessarily give the exact moment of collision.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.340
Sediment containing fragments eroded from a neighboring terrane shows proximity by

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.540
the time of deposition. Fossils can constrain former settings and connections,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.820
but an animal's ability to travel matters.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.100
Magnetic directions can reveal differences in original latitude or orientation.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.360
Similar ages alone do not prove two rocks formed together, especially

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.200
when their environments and histories differ.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:32.350
Alaska combines displaced continental pieces, oceanic material, arcs, and younger overlapping

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.110
deposits. Wrangellia is interpreted to have traveled north before colliding with

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.430
the western margin. Later strike-slip motion dispersed pieces along the coast.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.030
Matching sequences and ages connect those separated remnants.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:50.670
The sequence is formation elsewhere, transport, accretion, and possible later rearrangement;

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.690
the present map preserves the result.
